00:30:02.810 --> 00:30:04.810
what are some of your
favorite anime characters

00:30:04.810 --> 00:30:07.869
that you would like to cosplay, I guess?

00:30:07.869 --> 00:30:11.090
- I don't know, but-
- Like looks aside,

00:30:11.090 --> 00:30:13.010
just anyone that you like.

00:30:13.010 --> 00:30:15.330
- Ah, I don't know.
- I'm putting you on the spot

00:30:15.330 --> 00:30:16.410
here, I just realized.
- Do you watch much anime

00:30:16.410 --> 00:30:17.490
now still?

00:30:17.490 --> 00:30:19.680
- Lately, I haven't
been able to watch much,

00:30:19.680 --> 00:30:21.760
but I've always like in the past,

00:30:21.760 --> 00:30:24.217
one of my favorite series have been like

00:30:24.217 --> 00:30:25.367
"Full Metal Alchemist."

00:30:26.800 --> 00:30:27.996
- My favorite, my favorite.

00:30:27.996 --> 00:30:28.967
- Garnt is notorious for not-
- Really?

00:30:28.967 --> 00:30:31.613
- He's notorious for
not having watched it.

00:30:32.750 --> 00:30:34.909
As he should, 'cause
it's an amazing anime.

00:30:34.909 --> 00:30:37.116
- It's all right.

00:30:37.116 --> 00:30:37.976
- We're not gonna get into that.

00:30:37.976 --> 00:30:38.876
- Yeah, we're not gonna get into that.

00:30:38.876 --> 00:30:41.010
- But I usually get into
anime through the music.

00:30:41.010 --> 00:30:42.430
I've always been a music lover.

00:30:42.430 --> 00:30:45.730
So any anime with really
good music hooks me on,

00:30:45.730 --> 00:30:46.770
and then I start watching it.

00:30:46.770 --> 00:30:50.270
But I usually watch sort
of like adventure series

00:30:50.270 --> 00:30:54.200
where the main character
is like a guy or a boy or,

00:30:54.200 --> 00:30:55.890
not very like moe moe series-

00:30:55.890 --> 00:30:56.723
- Right, right, right, right.

00:30:56.723 --> 00:30:58.020
- I feel as well, like it made sense

00:30:58.020 --> 00:30:58.853
with "Full Metal Alchemist,"

00:30:58.853 --> 00:31:01.183
'cause the openings in that are bangin'

00:31:01.183 --> 00:31:02.658
So many good openings-

00:31:02.658 --> 00:31:04.130
- I haven't seen "Brotherhood" yet,

00:31:04.130 --> 00:31:06.480
but like the first "Brotherhood" opening

00:31:06.480 --> 00:31:08.550
is fuckin' legendary.
- Oh yeah, and also, you know,

00:31:08.550 --> 00:31:11.240
the Asian Kung-fu
Generation opening as well.

00:31:11.240 --> 00:31:13.663
Like yeah, it's so much
good music in that.

00:31:13.663 --> 00:31:16.690
- When I listen to anime opening songs,

00:31:16.690 --> 00:31:19.210
I can't figure out why I like
them, I just like the noise.

00:31:19.210 --> 00:31:22.150
I'm curious if you have like a,

00:31:22.150 --> 00:31:23.673
like, you must have an ear for it, right?

00:31:23.673 --> 00:31:25.570
What makes like a good anime opening

00:31:25.570 --> 00:31:27.470
in your opinion, I'm curious?

00:31:27.470 --> 00:31:28.573
- If it's sung by me.

00:31:30.236 --> 00:31:32.830
- Yours aside.
- You can say that as well.

00:31:32.830 --> 00:31:34.623
- I'll keep that as a memo.

00:31:35.870 --> 00:31:38.000
No, you know, I used to listen

00:31:38.000 --> 00:31:40.450
to anime openings before I made a debut.

00:31:40.450 --> 00:31:44.200
So what sort of hooked
me on was, you know,

00:31:44.200 --> 00:31:46.960
number one, it's really catchy.

00:31:46.960 --> 00:31:49.080
Number two, you know,

00:31:49.080 --> 00:31:53.383
it captures the mood
of the anime very well.

00:31:54.530 --> 00:31:57.630
And you know, I don't know, but number,

00:31:57.630 --> 00:32:00.660
I don't, what makes an anime opening-

00:32:00.660 --> 00:32:02.120
- I don't know, 'cause I have no clue.

00:32:02.120 --> 00:32:04.200
I thought maybe you could answer that.

00:32:04.200 --> 00:32:06.370
- I feel as though you
kind of answered that.

00:32:06.370 --> 00:32:09.750
It is all about, like if
you just personally like it,

00:32:09.750 --> 00:32:13.220
it sticks in your head, and
if it fits the theme, right?

00:32:13.220 --> 00:32:14.920
- Usually though, you know,

00:32:14.920 --> 00:32:18.370
if it's sort of, what do you call it?

00:32:18.370 --> 00:32:23.370
Like kose deki, you know, individual then-

00:32:23.393 --> 00:32:25.740
- Unique?
- Unique, yeah.

00:32:25.740 --> 00:32:27.170
Then it sort of stands out.

00:32:27.170 --> 00:32:28.900
And when something stands out,

00:32:28.900 --> 00:32:31.430
then like it catches your attention.

00:32:31.430 --> 00:32:33.719
- Yeah, because I remember
when I was in school,

00:32:33.719 --> 00:32:34.807
I watched a load of anime

00:32:34.807 --> 00:32:37.300
and people would ask me the question of,

00:32:37.300 --> 00:32:39.210
what are some, what are
the music you listen to?

00:32:39.210 --> 00:32:40.043
Who are your favorite artists?

00:32:40.043 --> 00:32:41.930
- And I go through like
my iPod or something

00:32:41.930 --> 00:32:44.083
and it's just all fuckin' anime openings.

00:32:45.180 --> 00:32:46.013
- Pass me the (indistinct).

00:32:46.013 --> 00:32:47.700
- And I'm just like,
oh, there's this really

00:32:47.700 --> 00:32:49.820
like underground Japanese band

00:32:49.820 --> 00:32:52.452
called like Asian Kung-Fu Generation.

00:32:52.452 --> 00:32:55.150
You probably haven't heard of them.

00:32:55.150 --> 00:32:56.500
- Look, that was the thing though, right?

00:32:56.500 --> 00:32:58.100
It's like there was
some kind of like level

00:32:58.100 --> 00:33:01.180
of like superiority in the
sense being like, yeah,

00:33:01.180 --> 00:33:03.380
yes, I listen to
underground Japanese music

00:33:03.380 --> 00:33:05.280
that you've probably never heard of.

00:33:05.280 --> 00:33:07.280
- But now, I feel like everyone listens

00:33:07.280 --> 00:33:09.270
to anime openings or anime music

00:33:09.270 --> 00:33:11.830
'cause everyone figures
out that it slaps hard, so-

00:33:11.830 --> 00:33:14.560
- Now, the superiority
is listening to a band

00:33:14.560 --> 00:33:15.610
that's done an anime opening,

00:33:15.610 --> 00:33:17.740
but listening to all the songs
that aren't in the anime.

00:33:17.740 --> 00:33:19.630
- Exactly.
- That are like

00:33:19.630 --> 00:33:20.930
every other song on the album.

00:33:20.930 --> 00:33:22.540
It's like, you only know the opening,

00:33:22.540 --> 00:33:24.023
I know the rest of the album.

00:33:25.010 --> 00:33:27.220
- It's like, I've recently gotten into

00:33:27.220 --> 00:33:28.870
like a lot of different artists

00:33:28.870 --> 00:33:31.640
who are like in J-pop and J-rock

00:33:31.640 --> 00:33:33.730
who have not been in anime openings

00:33:33.730 --> 00:33:35.160
or have not done anime music,

00:33:35.160 --> 00:33:38.980
but sound like they should
have done anime music.

00:33:38.980 --> 00:33:41.790
And I feel like a lot of
those people and bands

00:33:41.790 --> 00:33:44.330
really came up from
the Vocaloid community.

00:33:44.330 --> 00:33:46.340
And I don't know, like that's why,

00:33:46.340 --> 00:33:48.460
there's so much I don't know
about the Vocaloid community,

00:33:48.460 --> 00:33:50.870
but everyone seems to have
come out of that community

00:33:50.870 --> 00:33:53.900
and have this kind of genre of music

00:33:53.900 --> 00:33:56.000
that just kind of appeals to weeb.

00:33:56.000 --> 00:33:58.530
I can't like quantify in words,

00:33:58.530 --> 00:34:00.542
but it's the kind of
music that I just love.

00:34:00.542 --> 00:34:02.650
And I don't know what that
genre is, maybe utaites.

00:34:02.650 --> 00:34:04.020
- Yeah, I guess it's like, yeah,

00:34:04.020 --> 00:34:05.340
I know what you mean though.

00:34:05.340 --> 00:34:08.750
It's like, even if the genre
is completely different,

00:34:08.750 --> 00:34:10.520
there's just kind of like a stylistic

00:34:10.520 --> 00:34:13.250
or like something about
it where it's like,

00:34:13.250 --> 00:34:15.090
that sounds like it belongs in an anime.

00:34:15.090 --> 00:34:19.300
Like you can very quickly
tell with with Japanese music,

00:34:19.300 --> 00:34:21.220
if something is like made for anime

00:34:21.220 --> 00:34:23.060
and something's not made for anime.

00:34:23.060 --> 00:34:25.610
There's some quality about
it that I can't grasp

00:34:25.610 --> 00:34:27.523
where it's like, that you can
tell the difference in that.

00:34:27.523 --> 00:34:30.590
- But one thing I did
realize after making a debut

00:34:30.590 --> 00:34:35.417
as a sort of anime singer
was that anime songs and,

00:34:36.900 --> 00:34:39.590
well, I don't know how to say,
but regular J-pop or J-rock,

00:34:39.590 --> 00:34:42.110
there is a distinct difference.

00:34:42.110 --> 00:34:46.960
And you know, a lot of people
tend to like faster pace,

00:34:46.960 --> 00:34:50.027
really like energetic songs in animes,

00:34:50.027 --> 00:34:51.410
'cause you're limited

00:34:51.410 --> 00:34:54.350
to one minute and 30
seconds of the opening

00:34:54.350 --> 00:34:55.810
and it has to catch your attention.

00:34:55.810 --> 00:34:59.650
So usually, the ballads
are saved for endings

00:34:59.650 --> 00:35:01.300
and the really fast pace.

00:35:01.300 --> 00:35:04.563
Which is why I started
seeing like BPM 200.

00:35:05.530 --> 00:35:06.923
I'm just like dying in the studio.

00:35:06.923 --> 00:35:09.411
'Cause I'd never sung BPM 200,

00:35:09.411 --> 00:35:11.770
and after that, all my songs

00:35:11.770 --> 00:35:13.583
became like really high pace sort of.

00:35:13.583 --> 00:35:16.130
- I think I tried playing
some of your songs on osu!

00:35:16.130 --> 00:35:18.070
and my mouse couldn't keep up.

00:35:18.070 --> 00:35:19.020
I mean, what does that say?

00:35:19.020 --> 00:35:20.101
- It's just like instant-

00:35:20.101 --> 00:35:22.565
- I'm like, Jesus Christ,
this is going so fast.

00:35:22.565 --> 00:35:23.960
- Yeah so, I really don't recommend,

00:35:23.960 --> 00:35:26.780
like money-wise, yeah, please
do sing my songs at karaoke,

00:35:26.780 --> 00:35:30.620
but I don't recommend like
singing my songs at karaoke

00:35:30.620 --> 00:35:33.353
because of the sort of
level of difficulty.

00:35:33.353 --> 00:35:34.520
- I mean, if you're having
a hard time singing them,

00:35:34.520 --> 00:35:35.690
I don't know how some people,

00:35:35.690 --> 00:35:39.088
how us peasant normies are
gonna be able to sing it.

00:35:39.088 --> 00:35:40.940
- I personally would not
sing my songs at karaoke.

00:35:40.940 --> 00:35:42.530
- It's like when you get like two drinks,

00:35:42.530 --> 00:35:44.520
and you're like, yeah,
I think I can do Eminem.

00:35:44.520 --> 00:35:46.141
And then you do it, and you're like,

00:35:46.141 --> 00:35:47.930
I didn't remember this many lyrics.

00:35:47.930 --> 00:35:48.763
Where were all these,

00:35:48.763 --> 00:35:49.880
I thought it's just Slim Shady stand up.

00:35:49.880 --> 00:35:51.380
What is this?
- It's not just Eminem.

00:35:51.380 --> 00:35:54.930
It's like the really long,
like four verses Eminem.

00:35:54.930 --> 00:35:56.770
- It's seven minutes long, what?

00:35:56.770 --> 00:35:58.610
I thought it was three.

00:35:58.610 --> 00:36:00.760
- Alternatively, where you
sing, you choose a song,

00:36:00.760 --> 00:36:01.887
and you think you know the lyrics,

00:36:01.887 --> 00:36:03.560
and then you realize you only know

00:36:03.560 --> 00:36:06.110
about two sentences from the chorus.

00:36:06.110 --> 00:36:07.610
And then there's just
this awkward one minute

00:36:07.610 --> 00:36:09.980
where you're just kinda like
humming along to the verse,

00:36:09.980 --> 00:36:12.703
even though you have-
(Joey stutters)

00:36:12.703 --> 00:36:14.230
- Is it annoying being a singer

00:36:14.230 --> 00:36:15.530
and then having to go to karaoke?

00:36:15.530 --> 00:36:16.363
'Cause they expect you

00:36:16.363 --> 00:36:19.017
to like kill everything
instead of just shouting.

00:36:19.017 --> 00:36:23.040
- I have a secret, I sang
my own songs at karaoke,

00:36:23.040 --> 00:36:28.040
and you know, they have
a really weird system

00:36:28.230 --> 00:36:30.110
where they-
- Like the midi soundtracks?

00:36:30.110 --> 00:36:31.460
- No, no, they have this weird system

00:36:31.460 --> 00:36:34.924
where they like to give you
a score of how good you sing.

00:36:34.924 --> 00:36:36.470
- I turn that off, I don't wanna know.

00:36:36.470 --> 00:36:40.313
- I sang my own songs
and I got like a 60%.

00:36:42.847 --> 00:36:43.967
So I was like (laughs)-

00:36:45.227 --> 00:36:47.120
- Someone out there is
higher on the leaderboard.

00:36:47.120 --> 00:36:48.763
- Exactly, I'm like, wow.

00:36:48.763 --> 00:36:51.130
- It's like, when Charlie Chaplin entered

00:36:51.130 --> 00:36:54.800
like a Charlie Chaplin lookalike
contest and came in third.

00:36:54.800 --> 00:36:56.150
That's like the equivalent.

00:36:57.250 --> 00:37:00.310
- The points where they
score you are really,

00:37:00.310 --> 00:37:01.340
are very distinct.

00:37:01.340 --> 00:37:06.340
And I tend to do arrangements
and do stuff with my songs.

00:37:06.593 --> 00:37:09.640
I don't sing it exactly
like I did at the studio.

00:37:09.640 --> 00:37:13.050
So that automatically
like score things lower-

00:37:13.050 --> 00:37:14.450
- Those games do like the pitch,

00:37:14.450 --> 00:37:15.660
it's truly off pitch, right?

00:37:15.660 --> 00:37:17.820
So it's just like, you can just go, oh,

00:37:17.820 --> 00:37:19.513
and it'll like, it'll
give you like perfect-

00:37:19.513 --> 00:37:21.463
- It's just like very basic sing stock.

00:37:22.360 --> 00:37:23.650
I don't how how they're scored.

00:37:23.650 --> 00:37:26.050
I'm a bad singer, so I
I've never scored well

00:37:26.050 --> 00:37:27.950
on any of those kinds of karaoke games.

00:37:27.950 --> 00:37:29.965
- Well, I turn it off, I don't wanna know.

00:37:29.965 --> 00:37:31.080
I don't wanna know how
bad I am at singing.

00:37:31.080 --> 00:37:31.913
It's very bad.

00:37:31.913 --> 00:37:32.910
- Have you ever been in the group

00:37:32.910 --> 00:37:35.980
who hasn't known that you are a singer,

00:37:35.980 --> 00:37:37.600
and then you just start
singing your own song,

00:37:37.600 --> 00:37:38.840
and they're like, wait a minute.

00:37:38.840 --> 00:37:42.019
You sound just like that
person singing the song.

00:37:42.019 --> 00:37:45.056
- Nah, can't be, it's like 60%.

00:37:45.056 --> 00:37:47.410
- Actually, ever since I made a debut,

00:37:47.410 --> 00:37:49.440
I haven't been to karaoke
because it became,

00:37:49.440 --> 00:37:51.100
singing became my career.

00:37:51.100 --> 00:37:55.273
Like, it's difficult to do it for fun now.

00:37:56.460 --> 00:37:58.380
- I mean, it's just work for you and it's-

00:37:58.380 --> 00:37:59.660
- And when I start singing,

00:37:59.660 --> 00:38:02.020
I automatically feel like I'm on stage.

00:38:02.020 --> 00:38:05.093
So I think that's a little bit
heavy for the karaoke goers.

00:38:06.076 --> 00:38:07.690
- So I thought we're having fun, what?

00:38:07.690 --> 00:38:10.235
- Having a private concert right here.

00:38:10.235 --> 00:38:11.580
That's a little bit of a flex, right?

00:38:11.580 --> 00:38:14.070
Going to a karaoke and
singing your own songs.

00:38:14.070 --> 00:38:15.896
That's a bit of a flex.
- So if I do happen to go,

00:38:15.896 --> 00:38:19.420
then I usually am the one
like just putting everyone's

00:38:19.420 --> 00:38:21.070
songs into the machine.
- Tambourine.

00:38:21.070 --> 00:38:22.870
- Yeah, I love doing the tambourine.

00:38:23.780 --> 00:38:26.110
- I guess though it would
be such a weird pressure

00:38:26.110 --> 00:38:27.520
to go to karaoke with you.

00:38:27.520 --> 00:38:29.170
You absolutely kill
your songs and be like,

00:38:29.170 --> 00:38:31.070
oh, fuck, now I got to do "Sexy Back."

00:38:32.212 --> 00:38:33.773
It's just the difference.

00:38:35.000 --> 00:38:38.160
- Yeah.
- Yeah, I wanna know,

00:38:38.160 --> 00:38:40.480
when you started doing the anime openings,

00:38:40.480 --> 00:38:42.100
how did that affect the album

00:38:42.100 --> 00:38:43.750
that was already out and how like your,

00:38:43.750 --> 00:38:45.560
I guess your like level
of fame, if you will,

00:38:45.560 --> 00:38:46.650
and how it all kind of,

00:38:46.650 --> 00:38:49.410
did you see any like results
from doing anime opening?

00:38:49.410 --> 00:38:53.280
- Definitely, I think my
fan base sort of changed.

00:38:53.280 --> 00:38:56.370
Of course, my original
fans stuck with me loyally,

00:38:56.370 --> 00:38:57.990
very loyally.
- As they normally do

00:38:57.990 --> 00:38:59.480
in Japan.
- Yes, exactly.

00:38:59.480 --> 00:39:01.340
Which is really an amazing thing.

00:39:01.340 --> 00:39:02.650
But at the same time, like,

00:39:02.650 --> 00:39:05.330
I started out with my fan
base being mostly girls,

00:39:05.330 --> 00:39:06.950
because on Niconico,

00:39:06.950 --> 00:39:11.080
I think a lot of the fan base
was very like female-based.

00:39:11.080 --> 00:39:13.900
My fan base was female-based.

00:39:13.900 --> 00:39:16.180
But after I made an anime debut,

00:39:16.180 --> 00:39:19.840
so "BTOOOM!," "Arpeggio,"

00:39:19.840 --> 00:39:23.370
that made my sort of male fan base grow.

00:39:24.297 --> 00:39:25.130
And that was huge.

00:39:25.130 --> 00:39:27.320
When I went overseas and did concerts,

00:39:27.320 --> 00:39:29.910
I was really happy to
see that my fan base,

00:39:29.910 --> 00:39:33.520
my audience was kind of like a hybrid.

00:39:33.520 --> 00:39:35.820
Everyone was there, not just like females-

00:39:35.820 --> 00:39:36.780
- Like anyone can enjoy it.

00:39:36.780 --> 00:39:37.613
- Yeah, exactly.

00:39:37.613 --> 00:39:40.680
And so, you know, and I always
have loved rock and roll.

00:39:40.680 --> 00:39:45.680
So I think it's not necessarily
a gender-based music genre.

00:39:45.820 --> 00:39:48.470
So I was really happy
to see that my fan base

00:39:48.470 --> 00:39:53.470
was growing globally in
diversity, and so that was-

00:39:53.940 --> 00:39:55.383
- You kind of went through
a similar thing, right?

00:39:55.383 --> 00:39:58.080
Like you went from like a
primarily female fan base

00:39:58.080 --> 00:39:59.740
to a more of a hybrid now, right?

00:39:59.740 --> 00:40:01.217
- Yeah.
- Was it,

00:40:01.217 --> 00:40:03.270
was it like similar to
you where it's like,

00:40:03.270 --> 00:40:05.682
you kind of saw that like it diverse-

00:40:05.682 --> 00:40:08.393
- Well, he didn't have an
anime to jumpstart his-

00:40:08.393 --> 00:40:10.670
- Well, I mean, yes.
- He just went from-

00:40:10.670 --> 00:40:12.983
- He mostly jumped off one.
- Yeah, exactly.

00:40:13.890 --> 00:40:15.460
- So to sort of give you a rough example,

00:40:15.460 --> 00:40:17.970
I basically used to pretend
to be a British butler.

00:40:17.970 --> 00:40:20.400
Isn't that fun?
- Oh, that's,

00:40:20.400 --> 00:40:22.290
how do you pretend to be a British butler?

00:40:22.290 --> 00:40:25.330
- Well, you just turn up the
British accent more than usual.

00:40:25.330 --> 00:40:26.163
So you'd go like, oh,

00:40:26.163 --> 00:40:28.712
I suppose I could talk
like this, you know.

00:40:28.712 --> 00:40:31.950
- He was an actual living
anime avatar for a while.

00:40:31.950 --> 00:40:33.550
- Yes, I didn't use to
like show my face much,

00:40:33.550 --> 00:40:34.607
just do stuff like that.

00:40:34.607 --> 00:40:36.240
But anyway, that, as you can imagine,

00:40:36.240 --> 00:40:37.220
that kind of thing would attract

00:40:37.220 --> 00:40:38.700
more of a female audience than-

00:40:38.700 --> 00:40:40.797
- Exactly, which is
why fan base is female,

00:40:40.797 --> 00:40:43.800
because I didn't show my
face in the beginning.

00:40:43.800 --> 00:40:47.050
And my voice is pretty
low for a female singer.

00:40:47.050 --> 00:40:50.540
So a lot of people didn't know my gender.

00:40:50.540 --> 00:40:53.440
And so they assumed,
there was a huge fan base

00:40:53.440 --> 00:40:55.635
that assumed that I was a young boy.

00:40:55.635 --> 00:40:57.776
- Right, right, right.
- So you can imagine

00:40:57.776 --> 00:41:00.858
the shock.
- A shota boy.

00:41:00.858 --> 00:41:03.340
- You can imagine the shock
when I did my first concert,

00:41:03.340 --> 00:41:04.660
probably.
- Like, what the fuck?

00:41:04.660 --> 00:41:06.023
I wanted a young boy.

00:41:07.827 --> 00:41:10.530
- I'm sure there were
audience people like that.

00:41:10.530 --> 00:41:13.100
But you know, after I started
showing my face, though,

00:41:13.100 --> 00:41:15.560
my fan base started to
become more diverse,

00:41:15.560 --> 00:41:18.190
and not just to one genre.

00:41:18.190 --> 00:41:20.540
- How did, I mean, did you

00:41:20.540 --> 00:41:21.940
ever use to have fan interactions?

00:41:21.940 --> 00:41:23.090
And if so, how did that change

00:41:23.090 --> 00:41:24.360
when you went from that audience?

00:41:24.360 --> 00:41:26.450
'Cause I noticed a very distinct change

00:41:26.450 --> 00:41:29.900
in how my audience, I suppose,
behaves and talks to me

00:41:30.764 --> 00:41:32.190
when I went to more male.

00:41:32.190 --> 00:41:35.220
- Yeah, but personally, I didn't have

00:41:35.220 --> 00:41:37.563
much interaction with my fans until my-

00:41:38.870 --> 00:41:40.500
- Conventions.
- Yeah, my conventions

00:41:40.500 --> 00:41:41.333
and my lives.

00:41:41.333 --> 00:41:45.440
And so I don't really have like comparison

00:41:46.410 --> 00:41:50.220
between my Niconico
days and my debut days.

00:41:50.220 --> 00:41:52.700
But definitely, I love
being able to interact

00:41:52.700 --> 00:41:54.243
with like my listeners right now.

00:41:54.243 --> 00:41:55.390
This is amazing.

00:41:55.390 --> 00:41:58.650
I love being able to hear them,
hear their voices directly.

00:41:58.650 --> 00:42:00.800
And so like, I have no regrets

00:42:00.800 --> 00:42:04.170
about like going open with
my identity and stuff.

00:42:04.170 --> 00:42:05.350
- 'Cause do you feel
like it really hits you

00:42:05.350 --> 00:42:07.810
when you go to a convention
and you physically

00:42:07.810 --> 00:42:10.610
see the people that are in front of you

00:42:10.610 --> 00:42:12.000
that you're performing in front of?

00:42:12.000 --> 00:42:14.660
Because for us, YouTubers,
we see a number,

00:42:14.660 --> 00:42:16.000
and the number kind of just-

00:42:16.000 --> 00:42:18.139
- Doesn't mean anything.
- Means nothing.

00:42:18.139 --> 00:42:20.040
- You don't see a face.
- Yeah, we don't see a face.

00:42:20.040 --> 00:42:21.330
And then we try to like,

00:42:21.330 --> 00:42:23.560
even trying to quantify it
in our heads, it's just,

00:42:23.560 --> 00:42:25.510
it doesn't mean anything
until we go to a convention

00:42:25.510 --> 00:42:28.630
and we actually see the amount
of people in front of us

00:42:28.630 --> 00:42:31.330
and we're like, oh,
these people all know me?

00:42:31.330 --> 00:42:32.916
Jesus Christ, that's a lot of people.

00:42:32.916 --> 00:42:35.860
- It's kind of insane how
it's like a million views

00:42:35.860 --> 00:42:39.500
on a YouTube video seems so much smaller

00:42:39.500 --> 00:42:41.190
than physically seeing a hundred people

00:42:41.190 --> 00:42:42.327
in front of you.
- I know.

00:42:42.327 --> 00:42:44.500
- And it's such a weird
dichotomy how that works.

00:42:44.500 --> 00:42:46.640
And it's like, yeah, and it's like,

00:42:46.640 --> 00:42:48.030
we could be happy with all the views

00:42:48.030 --> 00:42:49.090
we get on all the videos,

00:42:49.090 --> 00:42:50.850
but at the end of the day,

00:42:50.850 --> 00:42:52.363
it doesn't really, you
can't see anything, right?

00:42:52.363 --> 00:42:54.130
That doesn't mean, it
doesn't really mean anything.

00:42:54.130 --> 00:42:55.010
I mean, it means something,

00:42:55.010 --> 00:42:57.210
but it doesn't mean as much as it should.

00:42:57.210 --> 00:43:00.130
- It's not as weighty-
- Yeah, exactly, exactly.

00:43:00.130 --> 00:43:02.380
- Before you debuted,
had you ever performed

00:43:02.380 --> 00:43:04.040
on a big stage before
or anything like that?

00:43:04.040 --> 00:43:07.522
- No, my first ever
concert in my entire life

00:43:07.522 --> 00:43:10.252
was in front of 2,000 people.
- Wow.

00:43:10.252 --> 00:43:11.085
Oh my God.
- A solo concert.

00:43:11.085 --> 00:43:14.603
So when the curtain
went up, I almost like-

00:43:14.603 --> 00:43:16.498
- Hi, guys.
- You know, I was like,

00:43:16.498 --> 00:43:17.915
my voice is gone.

00:43:19.677 --> 00:43:21.333
- Just went up two octaves.

00:43:22.250 --> 00:43:25.030
- So like I almost died on stage.

00:43:25.030 --> 00:43:25.990
- What's the biggest crowd

00:43:25.990 --> 00:43:27.680
you've ever performed in front of?

00:43:27.680 --> 00:43:29.813
- It was at an event, Anisama.

00:43:31.060 --> 00:43:33.878
So that was probably like ichiman,

00:43:33.878 --> 00:43:38.020
so 10,000-somewhat number of people.

00:43:38.020 --> 00:43:41.100
But when it becomes that sort of number,

00:43:41.100 --> 00:43:44.200
you can't see the faces, so
it's like a sea of lights,

00:43:44.200 --> 00:43:45.890
of like penlights and people,

00:43:45.890 --> 00:43:48.070
so you don't become nervous anymore.

00:43:48.070 --> 00:43:51.040
It's just like, it's more nerve-wracking

00:43:51.040 --> 00:43:54.797
when you're in a small venue
and you see every single face

00:43:54.797 --> 00:43:55.960
and they're all looking at you.

00:43:55.960 --> 00:43:59.900
- It's the eye contact that
really gets you, I find.

00:43:59.900 --> 00:44:01.400
- Because we don't have that experience.

00:44:01.400 --> 00:44:03.340
I mean, like, what's the
biggest amount of people

00:44:03.340 --> 00:44:04.687
you've done a panel-
- I'd say AX, right?

00:44:04.687 --> 00:44:05.520
- AX, I think.
- 3,000.

00:44:05.520 --> 00:44:09.340
- Garnt and I hosted in front
of like 3, 2, 3000 people.

00:44:09.340 --> 00:44:11.250
And that, I was shitting my pants.

00:44:11.250 --> 00:44:13.610
- Yeah, I hate it when
you just make eye contact

00:44:13.610 --> 00:44:14.730
with someone for like two seconds,

00:44:14.730 --> 00:44:16.830
and you're like, do you
reckon they noticed?

00:44:16.830 --> 00:44:17.940
They 100% noticed.

00:44:17.940 --> 00:44:20.077
That was that weird,
that was weird, right?

00:44:20.077 --> 00:44:22.360
- Can you imagine doing a panel
in front of 10,000 people?

00:44:22.360 --> 00:44:23.700
- I would like to not.
- Yeah,

00:44:23.700 --> 00:44:25.490
I would like to not either.

00:44:25.490 --> 00:44:27.610
- I got to ask, do you
ever get, is it like,

00:44:27.610 --> 00:44:29.630
do you ever get nervous
before going on stage?

00:44:29.630 --> 00:44:31.080
Do you ever get used to that?

00:44:32.050 --> 00:44:35.140
- I'm not a huge, like a nervous person.

00:44:35.140 --> 00:44:38.580
So I'm usually pretty like
excited to go on stage,

00:44:38.580 --> 00:44:40.470
and I'm like, I'm gonna have fun,

00:44:40.470 --> 00:44:43.290
woo-hoo, this is my home, you know?

00:44:43.290 --> 00:44:45.300
So I really don't get nervous.

00:44:45.300 --> 00:44:49.500
But when I do get nervous
though, it doesn't,

00:44:49.500 --> 00:44:51.770
it's every time, I do
get a little bit nervous,

00:44:51.770 --> 00:44:53.900
but I love being on stage.

00:44:53.900 --> 00:44:55.560
- Because I feel for me, it's,

00:44:55.560 --> 00:44:57.090
I don't know if it's nerves,

00:44:57.090 --> 00:44:59.475
but I just, it's the
waiting that kills me.

00:44:59.475 --> 00:45:00.380
I just wanna get on stage,

00:45:00.380 --> 00:45:03.086
whenever I have like a
panel scheduled or whatever

00:45:03.086 --> 00:45:03.919
and there's a lot of people,

00:45:03.919 --> 00:45:06.700
I just feel like once I get
on stage, I'm gonna be fine,

00:45:06.700 --> 00:45:08.750
but before that, I'm just restless.

00:45:08.750 --> 00:45:10.660
- It's like you're backstage,

00:45:10.660 --> 00:45:12.727
and you just hear the murmur of the crowd.

00:45:12.727 --> 00:45:14.480
That's when it's the most nervous.

00:45:14.480 --> 00:45:16.818
But the moment I start
like going up the stairs,

00:45:16.818 --> 00:45:18.960
it's just gone.
- That's exactly true.

00:45:18.960 --> 00:45:20.650
Like, I hate the waiting of it.

00:45:20.650 --> 00:45:22.530
The waiting is the most
nerve-wracking point.

00:45:22.530 --> 00:45:23.480
Once you're on stage,

00:45:23.480 --> 00:45:25.840
then you just gotta do what you gotta do.

00:45:25.840 --> 00:45:28.020
- Did you discover like the
first time you're on stage,

00:45:28.020 --> 00:45:29.250
was it like an aha moment?

00:45:29.250 --> 00:45:31.250
This is, I love this.

00:45:31.250 --> 00:45:35.100
- Oh, I thought, yeah, this is
definitely where I wanna be.

00:45:35.100 --> 00:45:37.310
I'm so grateful to have this chance

00:45:37.310 --> 00:45:39.060
to actually do what I love doing.

00:45:39.060 --> 00:45:43.760
And I don't know, it's just,
this is perfect for me.

00:45:43.760 --> 00:45:46.850
- Yeah, 'cause I assume it's
kind of like a trial by fire,

00:45:46.850 --> 00:45:49.480
'cause you said you had no
stage experience before.

00:45:49.480 --> 00:45:51.630
'Cause I can only imagine
some other artists

00:45:51.630 --> 00:45:55.278
who just kind of didn't
take the stage as well

00:45:55.278 --> 00:45:57.540
would have fallen flat.

00:45:57.540 --> 00:46:00.950
- I went on stage in
Japan for a video recently

00:46:00.950 --> 00:46:03.120
where I did like a visual kei video.

00:46:03.120 --> 00:46:06.050
And it was awful, I hated it.

00:46:06.050 --> 00:46:10.020
Because it was like 30 people,
so it was very intimate.

00:46:10.020 --> 00:46:13.860
And because, also COVID, no
one was allowed to like move.

00:46:13.860 --> 00:46:16.540
Everyone just had to stand,
which makes it even worse,

00:46:16.540 --> 00:46:18.038
'cause I wasn't doing anything.

00:46:18.038 --> 00:46:20.540
I was headbanging, but
anytime I stopped and made

00:46:20.540 --> 00:46:23.260
even like a millisecond of
eye contact with someone,

00:46:23.260 --> 00:46:25.110
and they were just like, I was like,

00:46:25.110 --> 00:46:27.670
oh my God, I'm awful, I'm terrible.

00:46:27.670 --> 00:46:29.070
I'm ruining the whole thing.

00:46:30.060 --> 00:46:32.870
Yeah, needless to say, I
was not happy about it.

00:46:32.870 --> 00:46:35.758
- It's like doing a performance
in front of your family.

00:46:35.758 --> 00:46:37.277
And they're just like being like-

00:46:37.277 --> 00:46:38.280
- But at least your family,

00:46:38.280 --> 00:46:39.690
you know your family's
gonna have your back

00:46:39.690 --> 00:46:40.640
no matter what.
- Right, right, right.

00:46:40.640 --> 00:46:43.020
- I figured out, when I have
to go on stage and do something

00:46:43.020 --> 00:46:45.240
to actually be good at it,
that's when I get nervous.

00:46:45.240 --> 00:46:47.292
'Cause I'm like, people have expectations.

00:46:47.292 --> 00:46:49.042
When you know that it's
okay to be bad at it,

00:46:49.042 --> 00:46:52.282
and like, that's the whole
joke, yeah, I like that,

00:46:52.282 --> 00:46:53.580
because it's like, all right,
I'm just gonna be a clown.

00:46:53.580 --> 00:46:54.640
That's what they came here for,

00:46:54.640 --> 00:46:55.550
so that's what I'm gonna do.

00:46:55.550 --> 00:46:56.940
- And I guess that's like the benefit

00:46:56.940 --> 00:46:58.280
of like doing a panel for Australia

00:46:58.280 --> 00:47:00.500
is that like most if not all the people

00:47:00.500 --> 00:47:02.830
who are attending it know us enough

00:47:02.830 --> 00:47:04.827
that even if we fuck up
slightly, they'll be like,

00:47:04.827 --> 00:47:05.660
ahaha, that's just

00:47:05.660 --> 00:47:06.823
him being him.
- Yeah, because

00:47:06.823 --> 00:47:07.656
I have no talent,

00:47:07.656 --> 00:47:09.473
so what am I gonna do on stage?

00:47:10.673 --> 00:47:12.880
- But I find, I've only really,

00:47:12.880 --> 00:47:14.960
my career took off in Japan,

00:47:14.960 --> 00:47:17.190
so I'm not an American-based singer,

00:47:17.190 --> 00:47:19.610
but at the same time,
I grew up in America,

00:47:19.610 --> 00:47:21.410
and I've been going to
concerts in America.

00:47:21.410 --> 00:47:23.050
And I did realize after my debut

00:47:23.050 --> 00:47:25.170
that the Japanese sort
of entertainment industry

00:47:25.170 --> 00:47:27.503
is completely different from the American-

00:47:27.503 --> 00:47:29.440
- 100%.
- 100% different,

00:47:29.440 --> 00:47:33.860
and the sort of expectations
in Japan are very high.

00:47:33.860 --> 00:47:35.540
You have to be perfect.

00:47:35.540 --> 00:47:39.120
A lot of people require
perfection as part of the job.

00:47:39.120 --> 00:47:41.880
So that is always very nerve-wracking

00:47:41.880 --> 00:47:43.940
to be able to have to sort of think,

00:47:43.940 --> 00:47:46.630
oh, I have to perform perfectly on stage,

00:47:46.630 --> 00:47:47.730
but that's impossible.

00:47:48.591 --> 00:47:49.820
- I feel like it's even
like when you're ordering

00:47:49.820 --> 00:47:52.180
a drink at Starbucks
here, they think like,

00:47:52.180 --> 00:47:53.150
oh my God, they're gonna hate me,

00:47:53.150 --> 00:47:54.641
I didn't put the little straw in.

00:47:54.641 --> 00:47:55.537
And it's like, no, no, no, it's okay.

00:47:55.537 --> 00:47:57.660
I feel so bad they're freaking out.

00:47:57.660 --> 00:47:59.930
But I mean, how is the difference

00:47:59.930 --> 00:48:02.680
between performing in
Japan and say, America?

00:48:02.680 --> 00:48:06.080
- You know, like in America, I
feel that when I go on stage,

00:48:06.080 --> 00:48:08.457
it's like a live, it's
really like a live thing.

00:48:08.457 --> 00:48:11.070
It's a live experience,
you're with your fans,

00:48:11.070 --> 00:48:13.370
you don't, nothing is planned.

00:48:13.370 --> 00:48:16.070
Your set list is planned,
but everything else is like-

00:48:17.190 --> 00:48:19.150
- Got a lot more freedom.
- Yeah, it's freedom.

00:48:19.150 --> 00:48:20.570
It's up to luck.

00:48:20.570 --> 00:48:24.310
But in Japan, I think even
the fans are very loyal

00:48:24.310 --> 00:48:26.510
in the sense that they really
want you to do your best.

00:48:26.510 --> 00:48:28.503
And they're always like
hoping you do your best.

00:48:30.100 --> 00:48:33.403
But the expectations are
completely different.

00:48:33.403 --> 00:48:34.810
I do love performing in Japan

00:48:34.810 --> 00:48:37.320
as much as I do love
performing overseas as well.

00:48:37.320 --> 00:48:38.680
But at the same time-

00:48:38.680 --> 00:48:40.040
- it's just a different experience, right?

00:48:40.040 --> 00:48:43.710
- Yeah, I feel that Japan
is more based on like,

00:48:43.710 --> 00:48:47.650
just doing things excellently
to the best as you can,

00:48:47.650 --> 00:48:50.200
always being, you know, just perfect.

00:48:50.200 --> 00:48:51.920
- Has anything bad ever happened on stage

00:48:51.920 --> 00:48:53.480
or something that you didn't predict,

00:48:53.480 --> 00:48:56.130
that you'd kind of just had
to deal with on the spot?

00:48:57.720 --> 00:49:00.450
- Luckily, I haven't had huge misses,

00:49:00.450 --> 00:49:05.440
but I'm always, the fear of
miss messing up is like here.

00:49:06.410 --> 00:49:07.692
- It's healthy, though, I think.

00:49:07.692 --> 00:49:09.380
You got to have that, right?

00:49:09.380 --> 00:49:10.949
If you're too complacent-

00:49:10.949 --> 00:49:12.168
- That's very true.
- I feel like you won't do

00:49:12.168 --> 00:49:13.620
your best.
- But it does appear

00:49:13.620 --> 00:49:15.283
as nightmares in my dreams often.

00:49:16.258 --> 00:49:18.591
Like I don't know how many-
- You wake up in a cold sweat

00:49:18.591 --> 00:49:21.320
and you're like, oh, what
if I fuck up on stage?

00:49:21.320 --> 00:49:24.210
- I don't know how many
times I've had nightmares

00:49:24.210 --> 00:49:27.880
where I completely messed up my lyrics,

00:49:27.880 --> 00:49:31.380
or like I don't even know
my own songs on stage,

00:49:31.380 --> 00:49:33.050
or like, I'm like what
am I doing on stage?

00:49:33.050 --> 00:49:34.300
I don't even know what to sing.

00:49:34.300 --> 00:49:37.990
I don't know, my lyrics
are completely washed out.

00:49:37.990 --> 00:49:41.700
So I think like somewhere in my head,

00:49:41.700 --> 00:49:46.390
I'm always like, that's a fear
of mine messing up on stage.

00:49:46.390 --> 00:49:48.270
- So like, how has, I suppose,

00:49:48.270 --> 00:49:51.220
how has COVID kind of
affected your schedule?

00:49:51.220 --> 00:49:54.080
What'd you like, what's a
day in the life of nano?

00:49:54.080 --> 00:49:56.955
What'd you do now that
you can't go on stage?

00:49:56.955 --> 00:49:59.790
- What are you up to, huh?
- What am I up to?

00:49:59.790 --> 00:50:04.100
But like I said, though,
as like a regular nano,

00:50:04.100 --> 00:50:07.490
not like a musician nano, I'm
a very stay-at-home person,

00:50:07.490 --> 00:50:10.720
so this is very, like,
not too different from my-

00:50:10.720 --> 00:50:12.600
- Comfortable, comfortable.
- Yeah, it's not too different

00:50:12.600 --> 00:50:13.670
from my-
- You're in your comfort zone.

00:50:13.670 --> 00:50:15.633
- But last year, in 2020,

00:50:15.633 --> 00:50:19.570
I had a lot of overseas
events planned, a lot of,

00:50:19.570 --> 00:50:22.960
my own tour was actually
canceled last year due to COVID.

00:50:22.960 --> 00:50:25.940
So in the music industry though,

00:50:25.940 --> 00:50:28.123
COVID has really taken its toll.

00:50:28.990 --> 00:50:30.090
And that's not just me,

00:50:30.090 --> 00:50:33.313
for all the musicians and
entertainers out there.

00:50:33.313 --> 00:50:35.490
So we're really struggling in that sense

00:50:35.490 --> 00:50:39.633
to be able to find new ways
to reach out to the audience

00:50:39.633 --> 00:50:42.330
and to give back to the audience

00:50:42.330 --> 00:50:46.810
even without being able to
directly interact with them.

00:50:46.810 --> 00:50:50.740
But that's, I think a
good sort of start for us.

00:50:50.740 --> 00:50:53.510
- Yeah, 'cause I assume
that being at home,

00:50:53.510 --> 00:50:56.140
have you had more time
to work on new music or-

00:50:56.140 --> 00:50:58.130
- Yeah, we've been doing
a lot of creating at home,

